41026ad1aef5cfd27a7c0ca679e1a"} {"question" "q-130301" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Of the given options, we consider the row operation \n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3}.\n$$\n\nApplying the above row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038539/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c0d6d44e1075987ba2cc1c738cd69eb3f141026ad1aef5cfd27a7c0ca679e1a"} {"question" "q-130301" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038539/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1d3886a32f041ff3d54ead8d7bedf6ee51066c77018660135ca1dc42931a9d61"} {"question" "q-130301" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038539/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c0d6d44e1075987ba2cc1c738cd69eb3f141026ad1aef5cfd27a7c0ca679e1a"} {"question" "q-130301" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}↔{R}_{1}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038539/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c0d6d44e1075987ba2cc1c738cd69eb3f141026ad1aef5cfd27a7c0ca679e1a"} {"question" "q-130301" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038539/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c0d6d44e1075987ba2cc1c738cd69eb3f141026ad1aef5cfd27a7c0ca679e1a"} {"question" "e-4693" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_2 := R_2 +(- 4) \\cdot R_1$ on $A?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038539/example-4693.json" "file_sha256" "cae1ed929ab7963fd45e5573559691f6a0b94a13cc3dc2ca0c4edf6a9546df53"} {"question" "e-4693" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The operation $R_2:=R_2 + (-4) R_1$ means that we need to add $-4$ times the first row to the second row. \n\nCarrying out this row operation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4+(-4)(1) & 2+(-4)(2) & -1+(-4)(3) \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -13 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038539/example-4693.json" "file_sha256" "cae1ed929ab7963fd45e5573559691f6a0b94a13cc3dc2ca0c4edf6a9546df53"} {"question" "e-4694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the augmented matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$ on $A?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038539/example-4694.json" "file_sha256" "c66245330d26e4ccb5cdae2c4ea864fac150efbb459ffcb1543059a64057d80b"} {"question" "e-4694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$ means that we need to swap the first and second rows.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038539/example-4694.json" "file_sha256" "c66245330d26e4ccb5cdae2c4ea864fac150efbb459ffcb1543059a64057d80b"} {"question" "e-4695" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which elementary row operation could transform the matrix $A$ into the matrix $B$, where \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038539/example-4695.json" "file_sha256" "c71b1e41799a028658e72182d16e31ab53fc4cba001e355a23e0c0ac265ea8fd"} {"question" "e-4695" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the $1$st and the $3$rd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has been changed is the $2$nd row. \n\nNow, applying the operation $R_2:=R_2+1 \\cdot R_1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4+1\\cdot (-4) & 1+1\\cdot (-2) & 2+1\\cdot 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the transformation could be done by the operation \n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+1\\cdot R_1\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+R_1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038539/example-4695.json" "file_sha256" "c71b1e41799a028658e72182d16e31ab53fc4cba001e355a23e0c0ac265ea8fd"} {"question" "e-4696" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n$[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ which elementary row operation could make $a_{22}=0?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038539/example-4696.json" "file_sha256" "28425ca8a1e67989e8c1d8a22cdd3be17a8e0bb4cb2c49d51cecbb54c5df1c46"} {"question" "e-4696" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In the given matrix, we have $a_{22}=5$ and we want to perform an elementary row operation that makes $a_{22}=0.$\n\n- We *cannot* make $a_{22}=0$ by switching two rows, because there are no rows with $0$ as the second entry.\n\n- We *cannot* multiply the second row by $0,$ because we are only allowed to multiply rows by *nonzero* constants.\n\n- However, we *can* add a multiple of another row to the $2$nd row to make $a_{22}=0.$\n\nLooking at the first row, we see that $a_{12}=1.$ If we add $-5$ times this entry to $a_{22},$ we will get $a_{22}=0$ as desired. So, the elementary row operation that we need to use is\n\n$$\nR_2 := R_2 + (-5) \\cdot R_1.\n$$\n\nApplying this operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1+(-5)\\cdot 3 & 5+(-5)\\cdot 1 & 6+(-5)\\cdot (-2) \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -16 & 0 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the desired elemetary row operation is\n\n$$\nR_2 := R_2 + (-5) \\cdot R_1,\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\nR_2 := R_2 -5 R_1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038539/example-4696.json" "file_sha256" "28425ca8a1e67989e8c1d8a22cdd3be17a8e0bb4cb2c49d51cecbb54c5df1c46"}] "usage" {"e-4693" [] "e-4696" [] "q-10248" [] "q-10217" [] "q-10250" [] "e-4694" [] "q-20388" [] "e-4695" []} "basis" 1381 "retractions" [[17592186059887 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$, given by\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_{1}↔R_{2}$ on $A$?"] [17592186059886 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $M$, given by\n\n$M = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_{3}:=R_{3} + 3R_{1}$ on $M$?"] [17592186059881 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$, given by\n\n$M = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-1) \\cdot R_{3}$ on $M$?"] [17592186060123 :question/problem "Given that\n\n$C = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$,\nwhich of the following elementary row operations makes $a_{22} = 0$?"] [17592186059504 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}$\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_2:= R_2 +(-4) \\cdot R_1$ on $A$?"] [17592186059504 :question/worked-solution "The operation\n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2 + (-4) R_1\n$$\n\nmeans that we need to add $-4$ times the first row to the second row.\n\nCarrying out this row operation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-4) \\cdot R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 + (-4)(1) & 2 + (-4)(2) & -1 + (-4)(3) \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -13 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186059505 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $A$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$ on $A$?"] [17592186059505 :question/worked-solution "The operation\n\n$$\nR_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2\n$$\n\nmeans that we need to swap the first and second rows.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}R_{1}↔R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186059506 :question/problem "Which elementary row operation could transform the matrix $A$ into the matrix $B$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186059506 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st and the $3$rd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has been changed is the $2$nd row.\n\nNow, applying the operation\n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+1 \\cdot R_1\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + 1 \\cdot R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4 + 1 \\cdot (-4) & 1 + 1 \\cdot (-2) & 2 + 1 \\cdot 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= B\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the transformation could be done by the operation\n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+1\\cdot R_1\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+R_1\n$$"] [17592186059507 :question/problem "Given that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ which elementary row operation could make $a_{22}=0$?"] [17592186059507 :question/worked-solution "In the given matrix, we have $a_{22}=5$ and we want to perform an elementary row operation that makes $a_{22}=0$.\n\n- We *cannot* make $a_{22}=0$ by switching two rows, because there are no rows with $0$ as the second entry.\n- We *cannot* multiply the second row by $0$, because we are only allowed to multiply rows by *nonzero* constants.\n- However, we *can* add a multiple of another row to the $2$nd row to make $a_{22}=0$.\n\nLooking at the first row, we see that $a_{12}=1$. If we add $-5$ times this entry to $a_{22}$, we will get $a_{22}=0$ as desired. So, the elementary row operation that we need to use is\n\n$$\nR_2:= R_2 + (-5) \\cdot R_1\n$$\n\nApplying this operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-5) \\cdot R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 + (-5) \\cdot 3 & 5 + (-5) \\cdot 1 & 6 + (-5) \\cdot (-2) \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -16 & 0 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the desired elemetary row operation is\n\n$$\nR_2:= R_2 + (-5) \\cdot R_1\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\nR_2:= R_2 -5 R_1\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186056007 17592186056008 17592186056009 17592186056010 17592186056011 17592186056037 17592186056038 17592186056039 17592186056040 17592186056041 17592186056043 17592186056044 17592186056045 17592186056046 17592186056047 17592186057560 17592186057561 17592186057562 17592186057563 17592186057564] "immutable_fields" [[17592186056006 :answer-field/correct] [17592186056006 :answer-field/id] [17592186056006 :answer-field/key] [17592186056006 :